{"numberOfHits":1,"results":[{"id":"GO:0044403","isObsolete":false,"name":"biological process involved in symbiotic interaction","definition":{"text":"A process carried out by gene products in an organism that enable the organism to engage in a symbiotic relationship, a more or less intimate association, with another organism. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts.","xrefs":[{"dbCode":"PMID","dbId":"31257129"}]},"synonyms":[{"name":"commensalism","type":"narrow"},{"name":"host-pathogen interaction","type":"narrow"},{"name":"parasitism","type":"narrow"},{"name":"symbiosis","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiosis, encompassing mutualism through parasitism","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiotic interaction","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiotic interaction between host and organism","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiotic interaction between organisms","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiotic interaction between species","type":"related"},{"name":"symbiotic process","type":"related"}],"replaces":[{"id":"GO:0030383","type":"consider"},{"id":"GO:0042280","type":"consider"}],"subsets":["goslim_chembl"],"children":[{"id":"GO:0043903","relation":"regulates"},{"id":"GO:0085030","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0036377","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0044399","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0075733","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0051702","relation":"is_a"},{"id":"GO:0051701","relation":"is_a"}],"secondaryIds":["GO:0043298","GO:0044404","GO:0072519","GO:0085031"],"history":[{"timestamp":"2026-02-12","action":"Added","category":"XREF","text":"Reactome:R-HSA-9839923"},{"timestamp":"2024-09-05","action":"Added","category":"CONSTRAINT","text":"Bad taxon ID"},{"timestamp":"2021-11-15","action":"Deleted","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_generic"},{"timestamp":"2020-11-03","action":"Updated","category":"TERM","text":"biological process involved in symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2020-11-03","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic process"},{"timestamp":"2019-10-20","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A process carried out by gene products in an organism that enable the organism to engage in a symbiotic relationship, a more or less intimate association, with another organism. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts."},{"timestamp":"2019-10-20","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A process carried out by symbiont gene products that enables the interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts."},{"timestamp":"2019-06-20","action":"Updated","category":"TERM","text":"symbiotic process"},{"timestamp":"2019-03-16","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A process carried out by symbiont gene products that enables the interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts."},{"timestamp":"2019-03-16","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A process carried out by symbiont gene products that enables a symbiotic interaction with a host organism. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts."},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"A process carried out by symbiont gene products that enables a symbiotic interaction with a host organism. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts."},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SECONDARY","text":"GO:0085031 (commensalism)"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SECONDARY","text":"GO:0072519 (parasitism)"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Updated","category":"TERM","text":"symbiont process"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between species"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis, encompassing mutualism through parasitism"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"host-pathogen interaction"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between species"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"parasitism"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between organisms"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"commensalism"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"host-pathogen interaction"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction with other non-host organism"},{"timestamp":"2017-12-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between organisms"},{"timestamp":"2016-07-23","action":"Deleted","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_metagenomics"},{"timestamp":"2015-05-19","action":"Deleted","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_ChEMBL"},{"timestamp":"2015-05-19","action":"Added","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_chembl"},{"timestamp":"2015-05-11","action":"Added","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_ChEMBL"},{"timestamp":"2015-04-23","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2015-04-23","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The term host is usually used for the larger (macro) of the two members of a symbiosis. The smaller (micro) member is called the symbiont organism. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2013-04-18","action":"Updated","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_metagenomics"},{"timestamp":"2013-04-18","action":"Updated","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_generic"},{"timestamp":"2012-02-17","action":"Added","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_metagenomics"},{"timestamp":"2008-06-09","action":"Added","category":"SUBSET","text":"goslim_generic"},{"timestamp":"2008-05-13","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The term host is usually used for the larger (macro) of the two members of a symbiosis. The smaller (micro) member is called the symbiont organism. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2008-05-12","action":"Deleted","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The term host is usually used for the larger (macro) of the two members of a symbiosis. The smaller (micro) member is called the symbiont organism. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2008-04-01","action":"Updated","category":"RELATION","text":"is a GO:0044419 (biological process involved in interspecies interaction between organisms)"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction with other non-host organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between species"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between organisms"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between host and organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"host-pathogen interaction"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between host and organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction with other non-host organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between species"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between organisms"},{"timestamp":"2007-08-09","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"host-pathogen interaction"},{"timestamp":"2007-06-22","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction"},{"timestamp":"2007-06-22","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiosis"},{"timestamp":"2007-01-25","action":"Deleted","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between host and other organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-01-25","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between host and organism"},{"timestamp":"2007-01-23","action":"Updated","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The term host is usually used for the larger (macro) of the two members of a symbiosis. The smaller (micro) member is called the symbiont organism. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction with other non-host organism"},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between host and other organism"},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"host-pathogen interaction"},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Added","category":"SECONDARY","text":"GO:0043298 (symbiotic interaction with other non-host organism)"},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Added","category":"SECONDARY","text":"GO:0044404 (symbiotic interaction between host and other organism)"},{"timestamp":"2005-12-10","action":"Updated","category":"TERM","text":"symbiosis, encompassing mutualism through parasitism"},{"timestamp":"2005-09-19","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between species"},{"timestamp":"2005-09-19","action":"Added","category":"SYNONYM","text":"symbiotic interaction between organisms"},{"timestamp":"2005-02-03","action":"Added","category":"TERM","text":"symbiosis, mutualism through parasitism"},{"timestamp":"2005-02-03","action":"Added","category":"DEFINITION","text":"An interaction between two organisms living together in more or less intimate association. The term host is usually used for the larger (macro) of the two members of a symbiosis. The smaller (micro) member is called the symbiont. Microscopic symbionts are often referred to as endosymbionts. The various forms of symbiosis include parasitism, in which the association is disadvantageous or destructive to one of the organisms; mutualism, in which the association is advantageous, or often necessary to one or both and not harmful to either; and commensalism, in which one member of the association benefits while the other is not affected. However, mutualism, parasitism, and commensalism are often not discrete categories of interactions and should rather be perceived as a continuum of interaction ranging from parasitism to mutualism. In fact, the direction of a symbiotic interaction can change during the lifetime of the symbionts due to developmental changes as well as changes in the biotic/abiotic environment in which the interaction occurs."}],"xRefs":[{"dbCode":"Reactome","dbId":"R-HSA-9839923"}],"taxonConstraints":[{"ancestorId":"GO:0044403","ancestorName":"biological process involved in symbiotic interaction","relationship":"never_in_taxon","taxId":"4895","taxIdType":"NCBITaxon","taxName":"Schizosaccharomyces"}],"aspect":"biological_process","usage":"Unrestricted"}],"pageInfo":null}